      "title": "Unit 2 — The Light",
      "objective": "Understand what light is, how people perceive it, and how it behaves with materials and objects.",
      "lessons": [
        {
          "title": "2.1 What Is Light?",
          "duration": "11 mins",
          "description": "Welcome to Unit 2. Before we can design with light, we need to understand what it is, how it behaves, and how we measure it.",
          "content": "<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Opening</h3><p>Welcome to Unit 2. Before we can design with light, we need to understand what it is, how it behaves, and how we measure it.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>The Nature of Light</h3><p>Light is electromagnetic radiation — the narrow band our eyes can detect, from 380 nanometers (violet) to 780 nanometers (red). Different light sources emit different spectral distributions. A warm incandescent emphasizes red and yellow wavelengths. A cool LED emphasizes blue. This spectral content affects how colors appear in our spaces.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Units of Measurement</h3><p><strong>Luminous intensity</strong> (candelas) — light in a particular direction. <strong>Luminous flux</strong> (lumens) — total light output in all directions. Think of lumens as miles traveled, watts as fuel consumption.</p><p><strong>Illuminance</strong> (lux) — light landing on a surface. One lux = one lumen per square meter. This is what regulations specify. <strong>Luminance</strong> (candelas per m²) — light perceived by the eye. This is subjective — each observer perceives it differently.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Two Currents of Design</h3><p>There are two currents in lighting design. <strong>The regulatory current</strong> — meeting lux standards, calculations, compliance. <strong>The perceptual current</strong> — how the space actually feels. You can meet every regulation perfectly and still create a terrible space. Regulations deal with illuminance (what hits the surface). Perception deals with luminance (what the eye experiences).</p><p>We use both tools: technical calculations for compliance, plus on-site tests for perception. Measurements are made for regulations; perception is made for humans.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>The Importance of Testing</h3><p>You cannot design light entirely from behind a screen. Renders can be adjusted — levels tweaked, luminaires added. But that's not genuine light. Before installing any fixture, we request samples, test on-site, and show clients how fixtures will actually look. The sensation matters more than the specification.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Transition</h3><p>Join me next where we'll explore how the human observer sees and interprets light — the subject and the observer.</p></section>"
        },
        {
          "title": "2.2 The Subject, Observer",
          "duration": "8 mins",
          "description": "We've talked about what light is. But there's a critical piece: the person experiencing it. The observer is just as important as the light source.",
          "content": "<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Opening</h3><p>We've talked about what light is. But there's a critical piece: the person experiencing it. The observer is just as important as the light source.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>The Human Eye</h3><p><strong>Rods</strong> — sensitive to brightness, active in low-light (scotopic vision, night vision). <strong>Cones</strong> — sensitive to color, active in well-lit conditions (photopic vision, full color). Between them: <strong>mesopic vision</strong> — the transitional state at dusk, in dimmed restaurants. Most interior environments operate here, where perception is most complex and interesting.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Visual Adaptation</h3><p><strong>Light adaptation</strong> (dark to bright) — fast, ~5 seconds. <strong>Dark adaptation</strong> (bright to dark) — slow, up to 30 minutes. <strong>Chromatic adaptation</strong> — adjusting to different color temperatures. The transition from a bright lobby to a dim restaurant isn't accidental — it's designed. The moment of dark adaptation becomes part of the experience.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>The Active Interpreter</h3><p>The observer is not passive. The same room lit identically can feel different to a 25-year-old and a 70-year-old — the lens yellows with age, requiring more light. A designer designing only for their own eyes designs for one person.</p><p>Consider a bedroom: desk work needs high-quality directional light. Reading needs warm, focused light. Meditation may require near-darkness. Each activity demands different lighting.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Perception vs. Measurement</h3><p>Two rooms with identical 500 lux readings can feel completely different. Warm indirect light on wood feels cozy. Cool direct light on white feels clinical. Illuminance = quantity. Perception = quality. We design for quality.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Transition</h3><p>Next: what happens when light meets materials and objects — how surfaces react to light and why material selection is inseparable from lighting design.</p></section>"
        },
        {
          "title": "2.3 Light, Materials, Objects",
          "duration": "11 mins",
          "description": "Now comes the crucial third element: the surfaces and materials that light interacts with. Understanding material-light interactions is the foundation of every design decision.",
          "content": "<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Opening</h3><p>Now comes the crucial third element: the surfaces and materials that light interacts with. Understanding material-light interactions is the foundation of every design decision.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>How Light Behaves on Surfaces</h3><p><strong>Reflection</strong> — specular (mirror), diffuse (matte), spread (satin). <strong>Absorption</strong> — dark materials absorb more. <strong>Transmission</strong> — clear glass vs. frosted glass. <strong>Refraction</strong> — light bends between materials. Every surface does some combination simultaneously. Your job is to predict and orchestrate these interactions.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Material Properties Matter</h3><p>A <strong>matte black desk</strong> photographed from two angles shows completely different results — reflecting from above, absorbing from the side. <strong>White blackout curtains</strong> block exterior light but bounce interior light. <strong>Dark vinyl paint</strong> absorbs excess on sunny days but feels somber on cloudy days. You must visit your space at different times of day.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Texture and Directionality</h3><p>Directional light reveals texture. Diffuse light hides it. <strong>Chukum plaster</strong> lit from the front looks plain. Graze it — aim light parallel to the wall — and every grain casts a tiny shadow. The surface comes alive. At Madre Café, our chukum walls were the hero, lit with warm indirect wall-washing.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Your Material Audit</h3><p>For your project space, document: What is the material? What is the finish? What color? How does it react to light? Photograph under morning, afternoon, and evening light. This catalog becomes essential for fixture selection.</p></section>\n<section class=\"lesson-section\"><h3>Transition</h3><p>We've covered light, the observer, and materials. In the next unit, we begin conceptualization — shaping everything into a design concept.</p></section>"
        }
      ]
